    "created_at": "2017-05-04T12:16:28Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-05-04T12:16:28Z",
    "body": "> The main thing I'm wondering about is whether this abstraction is correct. I thought we had come to the conclusion that we didn't want a callback per document, but rather one for all similar-origin documents?\r\n\r\nDo you mean in https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/707? When would the list of tasks for similar-origin documents be processed? If it's at the step I'm now traversing all the documents, then couldn't one use the top level browsing context's document to achieve that kind of effect?\r\n\r\n> Also, should we just have fullscreen tasks here? Otherwise if lots of things end up queuing animation frame tasks their relative order is unknown.\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/707#issuecomment-267590563 is the last I thought about this. I suspect we'll be better off if other specs can use a primitive like this. If the order of the two algorithms that queue the animation frame tasks is not well defined, then there would already be a problem if they both \"queue a task\", fire events, or do anything allowing the order to be observed.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/html/issues/comments/299168360/reactions",
      "total_count": 0,
